= Cyborg — Simple =

A '''cyborg''' is a living organism whose functions are integrated with technology.

The word comes from '''cybernetic organism'''. Manfred Clynes and Nathan Kline introduced it in 1960 while discussing technologies that might help humans adapt to space.<ref name="clynes">[https://www.fermatslibrary.com/s/cyborgs-and-space Clynes, M. E. and Kline, N. S. (1960), “Cyborgs and Space.”]</ref>

A cyborg does not have to look like a science-fiction robot. Real organism–technology systems can include implanted medical devices, neural interfaces, or advanced prostheses.

== What makes a system cyborg-like? ==

The strongest cases involve close functional coupling.

Technology may:

* measure a biological signal;
* process information;
* stimulate tissue;
* control a prosthetic device;
* regulate a physiological process.

The organism may also adapt to the technology through learning.

The more the biological and technological parts participate in the same functional loop, the clearer the engineering case for calling the system cybernetic.

== Feedback ==

Cybernetics focuses heavily on feedback.

A simple feedback loop is:

<pre>
body state
→ sensor
→ controller
→ intervention
→ changed body state
→ sensor
</pre>

A controller can compare the measured state with a desired range and change its output.

Not every device needs closed-loop feedback, but feedback is historically central to the cyborg concept.

== Cochlear implants ==

A cochlear implant is a clear real-world example of integrated biological and electronic function.

An external microphone receives sound. A processor converts sound into a coded signal. An implanted receiver and electrode array electrically stimulate structures associated with the auditory nerve.

The FDA describes a cochlear implant as an implanted electronic hearing device designed to provide useful hearing sensations through electrical stimulation inside the inner ear.<ref name="fda">[https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/cochlear-implants/what-cochlear-implant U.S. Food and Drug Administration, “What is a Cochlear Implant?”]</ref>

A cochlear implant does not recreate ordinary hearing exactly. The nervous system learns to interpret an engineered signal.

== Prostheses ==

A prosthetic limb can become more tightly integrated with the body when it uses biological signals.

For example, a system may use electrical activity from muscles to control movement.

More experimental systems can use nerve or brain signals and return sensory information through stimulation.

This creates two-way communication between person and machine.

== Brain–computer interfaces ==

A brain–computer interface can record neural activity and translate it into commands for a computer or device.

Some systems can also stimulate neural tissue.

The important distinction is between:

* recording from the nervous system;
* stimulating the nervous system;
* doing both.

Two-way interfaces create especially strong cybernetic coupling.

== Restoration versus enhancement ==

Some devices restore a function affected by injury or disease.

Others may extend performance beyond an ordinary baseline.

The boundary between restoration and enhancement is not always clear.

A device should be evaluated by what it actually does, how safe it is, and whether it serves the user's goals.

== Cyborg does not mean robot ==

A robot is a machine.

A cyborg includes a living organism plus integrated technology.

An android can look human but still be entirely artificial.

A person with an implant can be cyborg-like without looking different from anyone else.

== Security and privacy ==

Connected medical devices can create cybersecurity risks.

A software or communication failure can become a physical safety problem if the device controls stimulation or another bodily function.

The FDA therefore includes cybersecurity in medical-device lifecycle risk management.<ref name="cyber">[https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/cybersecurity-medical-devices-quality-management-system-considerations-and-content-premarket U.S. Food and Drug Administration (2026), medical-device cybersecurity guidance.]</ref>

Privacy also matters because implanted and wearable systems can collect physiological or neural data.

== Autonomy ==

Users should have clear information and meaningful control over a device that affects their body.

Important questions include:

* Can the user turn it off?
* Who can change its settings?
* Who receives its data?
* How are updates installed?
* What happens if it fails?
* Can it be safely removed or replaced?

Technical performance is not the only measure of a good system.

== Cultural meaning ==

“Cyborg” also has a broader philosophical and cultural meaning.

Donna Haraway used the cyborg as a political and philosophical figure for questioning traditional boundaries between human, animal, machine, nature, and technology.<ref name="haraway">[https://doi.org/10.5749/minnesota/9780816650477.003.0001 Haraway, D. J., “A Cyborg Manifesto.”]</ref>

That metaphorical use should be distinguished from biomedical engineering.

== Intelligence and consciousness ==

An implant can improve a specific function without increasing general intelligence.

Being technologically augmented also says nothing by itself about consciousness.

The scientific claim should stay tied to the measured function.

